{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>Until the twenty-first day.</b> Rashi: why is it stated? Was it not already stated 'seven days'? Because it says 'days' — from where do we know nights? Scripture says etc. One may ask: how can we find it to say 'from where do we know nights'? For above it is written 'from the first day until the seventh day,' and even nights are implied. Rather, with difficulty one may answer: there are things that receive reward for doing them but no punishment when one does not do them, such as matzah on the first night. And there are things that do not receive reward for doing them but reward when one does not do them, such as matzah from the first night onward. Nevertheless, 'matzot — seven days you shall eat matzot' is written, meaning: if he ate matzah all seven days, he has fulfilled the verse 'seven days you shall eat matzot.' From where do we know [that] if he ate matzah all seven nights he has fulfilled the verse? Scripture says 'until the twenty-first day' in any case. 'From the first day until the seventh day' refers only to 'any eater of leaven.' <b>Until the twenty-first day.</b> So that you not say: the seven days that HaShem commanded to eat matzah in them — there is no strictness to make them at the end of the month or intermittently. Scripture says: 'on the first, on the fourteenth day of the month at evening you shall eat matzot, until the twenty-first day of the month at evening.'"], :book :exodus, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {0 "The abbreviations פרש״י, חז״ק, ת״ל, ע״י, מ״מ, and הקב״ה are resolved mechanically to 'Rashi,' 'one may ask,' 'Scripture says,' 'through/by,' 'nevertheless,' and 'HaShem' respectively; י״ל rendered as 'one may answer.'"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:57:20.078124039Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}